00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:03.600
THE BATTLE FOR CISCO:&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:21.720
These two right here stole Cisco from Stanford&nbsp;
University. While immoral, this is nothing out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:27.060
of the ordinary within the business world. For&nbsp;
example, Mark stole Facebook from the Winklevoss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:42.180
twins and Divya Narendra. Steve Jobs stole the&nbsp;
Mac from Xerox. And Bill Gates stole Windows also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:49.440
from Xerox. Facebook, Apple, and Microsoft are all&nbsp;
multi hundred billion if not multi trillion dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.560
companies today. So, clearly, the punishment&nbsp;
for stealing ideas isn’t all that high in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:01.080
business world. Worst case scenario, you might&nbsp;
have to pay $65 million to settle a lawsuit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:08.040
but this wasn’t the case with Cisco. You see, like&nbsp;
the other victims, Stanford wasn’t really able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.960
do anything about Cisco through the legal system.&nbsp;
They thought about filing criminal charges against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.960
the founders, but they figured that it would just&nbsp;
be a better idea financially and stress wise to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:25.080
just license the technology to Cisco themselves.&nbsp;
At this point, Cisco’s founders probably thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:31.380
that they were in the clear, but karma often works&nbsp;
in weird ways, and Cisco’s founders would end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:37.680
losing the entire company to predatory VC firms.&nbsp;
Normally, you might feel bad for these people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.500
but in this case, they really just got a&nbsp;
taste of their own medicine. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:51.300
it was them who brought in these VC partners, so&nbsp;
really, there’s no one to blame but themselves. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.820
far the worst part about all of this is that Cisco&nbsp;
actually ended up doing extraordinarily well under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:02.700
VC leadership. In fact, in the 10 years after the&nbsp;
founders were pushed out, Cisco would grow from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:08.160
being a couple hundred million dollar company to&nbsp;
being the world’s largest company. At least, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:14.400
got a massive pay day thanks to their stake in the&nbsp;
company right? Psyche, they actually sold their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.320
entire stake for peanuts when they were pushed&nbsp;
out of the company out of resentment. Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:26.340
they didn’t enjoy being on the victim side of&nbsp;
having Cisco stolen. So, here’s the insane story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:31.320
how this couple tried to pull a quick on Stanford&nbsp;
only to have a quick on pulled on themselves.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.340
STEALING CISCO:&nbsp;

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.620
Taking a look back, the story of Cisco can be&nbsp;
traced back to a couple named Leonard Bosack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:46.500
and Sandy Lerner. Naturally, they were both&nbsp;
highly educated individuals. Leonard had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.700
bachelors in engineering from the University of&nbsp;
Pennsylvania and a masters in computer science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:55.920
from Stanford. It was while he was pursuing his&nbsp;
masters that he got the opportunity to work on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.860
special university project. The project entailed&nbsp;
connecting Stanford’s computer science wing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:07.320
business wing through a network so that data can&nbsp;
easily be communicated between the two. Hey isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:12.600
that Cisco’s entire business mod..anyway, it was&nbsp;
also around this time that he met Sandy Lerner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.760
who was at the time pursuing her own masters in&nbsp;
statistics and computer science at Stanford. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.920
two would get married almost instantly and they&nbsp;
would both get jobs at Stanford after graduation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:28.260
Leonard would become the director of the computer&nbsp;
science wing for Stanford’s business school. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.160
Sandy would become the director of the computer&nbsp;
science wing for Stanford’s computer science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:38.520
department. Most couples would call it right here,&nbsp;
but that was easier said than done for Sandy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:43.740
Leonard. Due to their jobs, they were constantly&nbsp;
coming across the smarted engineers in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:50.340
and their latest inventions which created a lot of&nbsp;
temptations. One such temptation was the Blue Box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:56.640
created by Andy Bechtolsheim and William Yeager.&nbsp;
The Blue Box was a simple multiprotocol router,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.640
but back in the early 1980s, this was the voodoo&nbsp;
magic that was connecting Stanford’s various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:09.420
departments. Imagine what you could do with this&nbsp;
in the real world, but unfortunately, that wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:15.420
possible. Stanford had made it explicitly clear&nbsp;
that the Blue Box was for internal use only.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:21.660
This applied to even the original founders. In&nbsp;
fact, William Yeager claims that his request to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:27.720
commercialze the invention was outright denied.&nbsp;
But, it doesn’t seem like this denial put off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:34.020
Leonard all that much. In 1985, Leonard would&nbsp;
join forces with another Stanford employee named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:41.040
Kirk Lougheed and the two would basically rip off&nbsp;
Yeager’s software to create Cisco IOS. With that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:47.940
Cisco was born, but not everyone was happy with&nbsp;
the company. Stanford was naturally furious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:54.060
they had specifically told even a cofounder to&nbsp;
not commercialize the product. Yet, here they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:59.580
dealing with a couple of hot head recent grads&nbsp;
trying to rip off the tech. I don’t think you’d be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:05.580
surprised to hear that Leonard didn’t keep his job&nbsp;
at Stanford for much longer. Stanford would turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.020
around and threaten to file criminal complaints&nbsp;
against Cisco for theft of software, hardware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:16.200
and other intellectual property. But, after&nbsp;
thinking about it for some time Stanford decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:21.420
not to pursue this route. The reality was that&nbsp;
it would be Stanford who would have to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:27.840
years of lawsuits to prove their case just to win&nbsp;
a pity settlement from Cisco. So, Stanford made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:34.380
the smarter choice and decided to just license&nbsp;
the tech to Cisco in 1987. At this point, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:39.900
couldn’t stop Cisco from commericalizing the tech,&nbsp;
so they might as well profit from it. Leonard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:45.540
and Sandy probably thought that they were in the&nbsp;
clear, but they had yet to face the real predator.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:49.440
GROWING CISCO:&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:55.980
By 1987, just 2 years after the company was&nbsp;
founded, Cisco was pulling in nearly $2 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.980 --> 00:06:00.720
per month in revenue. Originally, the people&nbsp;
buying their network technology was research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:06.240
centers, government facilities, and universities.&nbsp;
But, slowly, more and more companies became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:12.780
interested and they started making big orders.&nbsp;
While this was great news for the company, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:19.020
also terrible news because fulfilling these orders&nbsp;
was much easier said than done. Unlike startups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:24.300
today who can just scale to the entire world using&nbsp;
insanely powerful cloud computers, Cisco had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:30.120
scale the old fashioned way. After all, they were&nbsp;
producing physical routers and networking gear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:36.120
so they had to deal with suppliers, manufacturers,&nbsp;
logistics, scaling, inventory, and all of that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:41.460
This is hard enough for a consumer company to&nbsp;
deal with. So, I think you can imagine how hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:48.480
it was for a startup to fulfill orders of 10,000&nbsp;
or 30,000 routers. So, Cisco really had no choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:54.540
but to raise venture capital. In 1988, they would&nbsp;
approach the famous Sequoia Capital to give them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:59.760
some extra cash to grow the company. Sequoia would&nbsp;
happily give them cash the first time around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:07.680
but this cash didn’t even last 12 months, and it&nbsp;
wasn’t long until Cisco was asking Sequoia for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:13.020
more which Sequoia wasn’t exactly comfortable&nbsp;
with. This was not long after black monday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:19.080
so the stock market wasn’t exactly performing&nbsp;
great. Not to mention, Sequoia had just gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:24.360
them money. For Sequoia to get even more involved,&nbsp;
they needed more assurance that this company would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:30.360
actually make it to the other side. So, they&nbsp;
proposed a deal. Sequoia would continue funding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:35.220
Cisco if Cisco gave them controlling voting&nbsp;
power and allowed for Sequoia’s founder Don&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:41.160
Valentine to become the chairman of the company.&nbsp;
Obviously, this was a pretty aggressive ask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:47.100
but Cisco had no time to shop around for more&nbsp;
money. It was either, take the deal with Sequoia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:52.440
or gamble with the company’s life. Leonard and&nbsp;
Sandy of course chose to take the deal, and from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:58.200
a financial perspective, it worked out great. By&nbsp;
1989, Cisco would be able to expand to 3 products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:06.120
111 employees, and a cool $27 million in revenue.&nbsp;
But, all of this of course came with a price. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:12.120
now had to deal with Don Valentine who wasn’t&nbsp;
exactly a friend. This isn’t to say that Don&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:18.480
didn’t have Cisco’s best interest in mind. The&nbsp;
problem was that that was the only interest he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:23.940
had in mind. Frankly, he didn’t care about Leonard&nbsp;
or Sandy. He had poured a lot of money into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:28.860
startup and he was gonna make sure that it panned&nbsp;
out. To increase the chances of this happening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:33.900
he would go ahead and hire a computer veteran&nbsp;
named John Morgridge as the company’s new CEO.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:39.720
Leonard would be demoted to the role of Chief&nbsp;
Scientist which wasn’t too bad. Sandy though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:45.060
was demoted to the role of wait for it:&nbsp;
leading customer service. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:50.700
Don Valentine was straight up insulting the&nbsp;
founders and things would only get worse.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:56.640
LOSING CISCO:
In February of 1990,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:04.080
Cisco would go public and enjoy a roaring IPO. The&nbsp;
company would close off the first of trading with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:11.100
a market cap of $224 million which made the&nbsp;
founders centimillionaires. Not a bad sum of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:16.500
money, but the company was just getting started.&nbsp;
They had just barely escaped the funding crunch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:22.560
and companies were just starting to leverage their&nbsp;
tech. So, they had a bright future ahead in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:29.580
billions and tens of billions. But, it seems that&nbsp;
Don may have had some ulterior motives when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:34.920
came to taking Cisco public. You see, Leonard and&nbsp;
Sandy now had an easy way to sell their stakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:40.200
so it became that much easier for Don to push&nbsp;
out the founders, and that’s exactly what he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:47.880
would do. On August 28, 1990, Don and John would&nbsp;
straight up fire Sandy. Leonard would instantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:53.640
resign thinking that it would be some sort of&nbsp;
protest, but Don didn’t give a crap. The reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:59.340
was that Cisco didn’t need Sandy or Leonard&nbsp;
to be successful. Afterall, they didn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:04.560
invent the technology. To make things worse,&nbsp;
Don and John felt that Sandy and Leonard were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:09.600
completely incompetent. In fact, during Sandy’s&nbsp;
first meeting with John, he would tell her quote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:16.680
“I hear that you're everything that's wrong with&nbsp;
Cisco." This bad blood only increased over time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:23.940
as John felt that the founders had no clue about&nbsp;
sales. He felt that the only reason Cisco was even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:28.920
able to grow so far was thanks to companies that&nbsp;
would go out of their way to have the latest and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:34.800
greatest. Cisco was by no means appealing to the&nbsp;
average company which is where the real money was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:40.560
And considering that Cisco would literally grow a&nbsp;
1000X over the next 10 years under his leadership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:46.380
it looks like he was right. So, to be honest,&nbsp;
when Leonard resigned, Don probabaly didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:52.020
think “Oh no, I lost a cofounder.” Instead, he&nbsp;
probably thought, “Great, two birds with one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:57.060
stone.” Leonard and Sandy should’ve just taken&nbsp;
the hint, stepped away, and enjoyed their lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:03.060
but they wanted to send Don and John a message&nbsp;
that they didn’t need Cisco either. And with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:08.160
they would go ahead and sell their entire 66%&nbsp;
stake in the company for $170 million in a fit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:15.420
rage. That may have felt good at the time, but&nbsp;
it was objectively a brain dead decision. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:20.640
it’s not like they needed the money or that they&nbsp;
didn’t believe in the future of the company. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:26.100
was literally nothing to this sale other than&nbsp;
ego and resentment, and they would be the ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:31.560
who ended up paying the price. 10 years later,&nbsp;
Cisco would become the world’s largest company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:39.960
with a market cap of $569 billion. This means&nbsp;
that they’re 66% stake would’ve been worth $375&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:44.280
billion. Even if we assume they sold off 46%&nbsp;
of their stake within the next 10 years, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:50.640
remaining 20% would be worth over a $100&nbsp;
billion. Yep, they done diddly do screwed up.

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:54.480
THE AFTERMATH:&nbsp;

00:11:54.480 --> 00:12:00.720
Shortly after the whole Cisco fiasco, Sandy and&nbsp;
Leonard’s marriage would also fall part and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:06.540
was really the rock bottom for these two. But&nbsp;
I do gotta say, they did pull themselves back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:10.680
up and focused on giving away the money&nbsp;
that they did recieve. They would donate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:15.900
70% of the proceeds to charities focused on&nbsp;
promoting animal welfare and science projects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:21.360
If you ask the duo about Cisco today, they have&nbsp;
some pretty diverse answers. Leonard plays it off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:26.160
pretty cool and simply says that they walked&nbsp;
away with a bunch of money anyway so there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:31.380
nothing to regret. As for Sandy, however, she’s&nbsp;
understandably still pretty triggered about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:37.740
situation. “Len and I under-estimated our skills.&nbsp;
I certainly could have run that business. I had my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:42.120
hands on the reins." Also, if you’re wondering&nbsp;
what happened to the founders of the blue box,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:48.180
well, Andy Bechtolsheim would go on to found&nbsp;
Sun Microsystems and he now boasts a net worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:54.600
of over $10 billion. So, looks like he&nbsp;
didn’t need the blue box to make it big.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.140 --> 00:13:01.020
The only ones regretting their choices today is&nbsp;
the ones who tried stealing. Do you think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

